cff-version: 1.1.0
message: "If you use this design or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "Drone-Stabilized Passive Dynamic Walker (open-source hardware)"
authors:
  - family-names: "Ishida"
    given-names: "Eiri"
abstract: >
  Six simulation-validated generations of a McGeer-type passive dynamic
  walker whose inverted-pendulum instability is tamed without sacrificing
  the passive limit cycle. Gen-1..3 stabilize attitude only from above the
  virtual pivot point: by drone thrust (Gen-1) or partial helium buoyancy
  kept within the 37-94% weight operating window so the legs remain loaded
  (Gen-2 spherical envelope, Gen-3 airship hull with cruciform tail);
  horizontal fan thrust replaces the slope as the energy top-up.
  Gen-4..6 descend from the karakuri "dankaeri" tumbling doll and walk by
  water transfer alone on mass geometry: water-ballasted feet with one-way
  half-kamaboko rocker soles and an escapement-quantized crank (Gen-4),
  crank-mounted tanks with hollow conduit arms and a hub pump/valve so the
  whole body somersaults dankaeri-fashion (Gen-5), and gear-locked
  parallelogram legs guided by twin same-phase cranks - the tank crank
  alone somersaults, the body stays upright, and the machine achieves
  zero falls over the whole pump range with an exact kinematic stride
  (Gen-6). Includes FreeCAD sources, engineering drawings, PyBullet
  physics/control code, gait trajectories, Blender rigs, and videos.
  Licensed under CERN-OHL-S-2.0 (hardware), MIT (code), and CC-BY-SA-4.0
  (documentation and media); see LICENSES/ in the repository.
keywords:
  - passive dynamic walking
  - buoyancy stabilization
  - virtual pivot point
  - karakuri
  - dankaeri tumbling doll
  - water ballast propulsion
  - parallelogram linkage
  - gear-synchronized parallel cranks
  - open source hardware
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21629977
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-07-28
